2
00:03:06,979 --> 00:03:08,814
Thank you very much

3
00:03:08,814 --> 00:03:10,649
and welcome
to To Tell the Truth.

4
00:03:10,649 --> 00:03:12,442
Our first guest,
he's made a career

5
00:03:12,442 --> 00:03:14,611
out of being
the most outrageous impostor

6
00:03:14,611 --> 00:03:16,655
that we've ever come across
on this show

7
00:03:16,655 --> 00:03:18,574
and you're going to see
what I mean.

8
00:03:18,574 --> 00:03:19,826
Number One

9
00:03:19,826 --> 00:03:21,493
what is your name, please?

10
00:03:21,493 --> 00:03:23,329
My name is
Frank William Abagnale.

11
00:03:23,329 --> 00:03:24,664
Number Two?

12
00:03:24,664 --> 00:03:27,000
My name is
Frank William Abagnale.

13
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:28,334
Number Three?

14
00:03:28,334 --> 00:03:30,502
My name is
Frank William Abagnale.

15
00:03:30,502 --> 00:03:33,130
"From 1964 to 1967

16
00:03:33,130 --> 00:03:34,756
"I successfully impersonated

17
00:03:34,756 --> 00:03:37,426
"an airline pilot
for Pan Am Airways

18
00:03:37,426 --> 00:03:40,680
"and I flew over
two million miles for free.

19
00:03:40,680 --> 00:03:44,349
"During that time, I was also
the chief resident pediatrician

20
00:03:44,349 --> 00:03:46,310
"at a Georgia hospital

21
00:03:46,310 --> 00:03:48,353
"and an assistant
attorney general

22
00:03:48,353 --> 00:03:50,063
"for the state of Louisiana.

23
00:03:50,063 --> 00:03:52,441
"By the time I was caught,
I was considered

24
00:03:52,441 --> 00:03:55,444
"the youngest and most daring
con man in U.s. history.

25
00:03:55,444 --> 00:03:58,865
"I had cashed almost $4 million
in fraudulent checks

26
00:03:58,865 --> 00:04:02,242
"in 26 foreign countries
and all 50 states.

27
00:04:02,242 --> 00:04:05,621
"And I did it all
before my 19th birthday.

28
00:04:05,621 --> 00:04:08,707
My name is
Frank William Abagnale."

29
00:04:08,707 --> 00:04:10,584
You don't know how
to tell the truth...

30
00:04:11,710 --> 00:04:13,629
You don't know how
to tell the truth...

31
00:04:13,629 --> 00:04:14,713
so, for the first time

32
00:04:14,713 --> 00:04:16,298
he's going to have
to tell the truth

33
00:04:16,298 --> 00:04:18,175
and we're going to start
our questioning with Kitty.

34
00:04:18,175 --> 00:04:21,386
Thank you. Number One,
why, with all your talent...

35
00:04:21,386 --> 00:04:23,723
and you're obviously
a very bright fellow...

36
00:04:23,723 --> 00:04:26,433
why didn't you go in
for a legitimate profession?

37
00:04:26,433 --> 00:04:28,977
It was really a question
of dollars and cents.

38
00:04:28,977 --> 00:04:31,313
When I was a young man,
I needed the money

39
00:04:31,313 --> 00:04:32,523
and I thought
this list of careers

40
00:04:32,523 --> 00:04:33,899
was the easiest way to get it.

41
00:04:33,899 --> 00:04:34,900
I see.

42
00:04:34,900 --> 00:04:37,861
Number Two, I find this all
very fascinating.

43
00:04:37,861 --> 00:04:39,571
Who was it
that finally caught you?

44
00:04:40,948 --> 00:04:43,075
His name was Carl Hanratty.

45
00:04:43,075 --> 00:04:44,911
Han-an-an-ratty.

46
00:04:44,911 --> 00:04:46,119
Ratty.

47
00:04:46,119 --> 00:04:47,579
Hanratty.

48
00:04:47,579 --> 00:04:48,580
Han-ratty.

49
00:04:48,580 --> 00:04:50,916
Carl Hanratty.

50
00:04:50,916 --> 00:04:52,252
- Yes.
- I...

51
00:04:52,252 --> 00:04:54,878
am... Carl Hanratty.

52
00:04:54,878 --> 00:04:59,258
I represent the FBI from
the United states of America.

53
00:05:00,926 --> 00:05:02,719
Yeah. I have orders

54
00:05:02,719 --> 00:05:05,597
to see the American
prisoner, Abagnale.

55
00:05:32,457 --> 00:05:35,961
You sit here.

56
00:05:35,961 --> 00:05:38,798
You do not open the door.

57
00:05:38,798 --> 00:05:42,467
You do not pass him...

58
00:05:42,467 --> 00:05:45,804
anything through the hole.

59
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:57,482
Aw... Jesus.

60
00:06:17,878 --> 00:06:20,130
You know, I've got
a little bit of a cold myself.

61
00:06:20,130 --> 00:06:21,924
- Frank...

62
00:06:21,924 --> 00:06:24,051
I'm here to read
the articles of extradition

63
00:06:24,051 --> 00:06:26,637
according to the
European Court for Human Rights.

64
00:06:26,637 --> 00:06:29,264
"Article One:
Extradition shall be granted

65
00:06:29,264 --> 00:06:31,517
"in respect of offenses
punishable under the laws..."

66
00:06:31,517 --> 00:06:32,852
elp me.

67
00:06:32,852 --> 00:06:35,062
"...for the maximum period
of at least one year

68
00:06:35,062 --> 00:06:36,856
of a severe penalty."

69
00:06:36,856 --> 00:06:38,524
Help me.

70
00:06:38,524 --> 00:06:41,693
Frank... stop it.

71
00:06:41,693 --> 00:06:43,987
Help me.

72
00:06:43,987 --> 00:06:46,615
You don't think actually
you can fool me, do you?

73
00:06:48,659 --> 00:06:50,953
16 pages to go.
stay with me.

74
00:06:50,953 --> 00:06:53,205
Article Two:

75
00:06:53,205 --> 00:06:54,748
"If the request for extradition

76
00:06:54,748 --> 00:06:56,500
"includes several separate
offenses

77
00:06:56,500 --> 00:06:57,876
each of which is punishable
under the laws..."

78
00:06:58,919 --> 00:07:00,254
"of the requesting party..."

79
00:07:00,254 --> 00:07:02,256
Frank?

80
00:07:04,383 --> 00:07:06,552
Frank?

81
00:07:06,552 --> 00:07:08,053
Goddamn it!

82
00:07:08,053 --> 00:07:10,722
- Get me a doctor in here!

83
00:07:10,722 --> 00:07:12,057
- I need a doctor!
- Yes.

84
00:07:12,057 --> 00:07:13,393
Doctor! Now!

85
00:07:13,393 --> 00:07:14,894
Don't sweat it, Frank.

86
00:07:14,894 --> 00:07:16,854
We're going to get you
right to a doctor.

87
00:07:20,148 --> 00:07:22,025
Frank, if you can
hear me, don't worry.

88
00:07:22,025 --> 00:07:24,069
I'm going to take you
home in the morning.

89
00:07:24,069 --> 00:07:25,404
Home in the morning, Frank.

90
00:07:29,741 --> 00:07:31,077
What are you doing?

91
00:07:31,077 --> 00:07:32,412
Washing off the lice.

92
00:07:32,412 --> 00:07:34,872
This man has to be on a plane
for America.

93
00:07:34,872 --> 00:07:36,248
He has to see a doctor.

94
00:07:36,248 --> 00:07:37,958
The doctor comes in tomorrow.

95
00:07:37,958 --> 00:07:40,002
I have worked too long, too hard

96
00:07:40,002 --> 00:07:42,087
for you to take this
away from me.

97
00:07:42,087 --> 00:07:44,590
If he dies,
I'm holding you responsible.

98
00:07:50,762 --> 00:07:52,181
Monsieur!

99
00:07:56,602 --> 00:07:58,353
Ah, Frank.

100
00:08:40,604 --> 00:08:42,356
Okay, Carl...

101
00:08:42,356 --> 00:08:45,484
let's go home.

102
00:08:45,484 --> 00:08:48,070
MAN:
The New Rochelle Rotary Club

103
00:08:48,070 --> 00:08:51,114
has a history
that goes back to 1919.

104
00:08:51,114 --> 00:08:52,574
In all those years

105
00:08:52,574 --> 00:08:56,453
we've only seen a handful
of deserving gentlemen

106
00:08:56,453 --> 00:08:59,331
inducted as lifetime members.

107
00:08:59,331 --> 00:09:01,333
It's an honor that, uh

108
00:09:01,333 --> 00:09:06,171
that has seen 57 names enshrined
on the wall of honor

109
00:09:06,171 --> 00:09:08,674
and tonight, we make it 5:

110
00:09:08,674 --> 00:09:13,345
so please stand as I present
my very good friend

111
00:09:13,345 --> 00:09:14,763
a man who keeps
our pencils sharp...

112
00:09:16,306 --> 00:09:18,017
and our pens in ink...

113
00:09:18,017 --> 00:09:20,519
Frank William Abagnale.

114
00:09:40,581 --> 00:09:42,207
I stand here humbled

115
00:09:42,207 --> 00:09:45,335
by the presence
of Mayor Robert Wagner...

116
00:09:49,840 --> 00:09:52,843
...and our club president,
Jack Barnes.

117
00:09:57,890 --> 00:10:01,518
Most of all, I'm honored to see
my loving wife Paula...

118
00:10:04,313 --> 00:10:07,858
...and my son, Frank Jr...

119
00:10:07,858 --> 00:10:09,818
Frank... Aw, stand up.
Come on...

120
00:10:13,488 --> 00:10:16,450
...sitting in the front row.

121
00:10:20,746 --> 00:10:25,584
Two little mice fell
in a bucket of cream.

122
00:10:25,584 --> 00:10:30,464
The first mouse quickly gave up
and drowned.

123
00:10:30,464 --> 00:10:34,843
The second mouse...
wouldn't quit.

124
00:10:34,843 --> 00:10:36,345
He struggled so hard

125
00:10:36,345 --> 00:10:39,973
that eventually he churned
that cream into butter

126
00:10:39,973 --> 00:10:41,892
and crawled out.

127
00:10:41,892 --> 00:10:46,146
Gentlemen, as of this moment,
I am that second mouse.

128
00:11:03,330 --> 00:11:06,041
You're a better dancer
than your father, Frankie.

129
00:11:06,041 --> 00:11:07,626
You hear that, Daddy?

130
00:11:07,626 --> 00:11:08,628
Like fun.

131
00:11:08,628 --> 00:11:09,795
The girls don't know

132
00:11:09,795 --> 00:11:10,838
what they're in for.

133
00:11:10,838 --> 00:11:13,924
show him the dance
you were doing when we met.

134
00:11:13,924 --> 00:11:15,301
Ah, who can remember?

135
00:11:15,301 --> 00:11:17,804
The people
in that little French village

136
00:11:17,804 --> 00:11:19,805
were so happy to see Americans

137
00:11:19,805 --> 00:11:22,140
they decided
to put on a show for us.

138
00:11:22,140 --> 00:11:24,142
so they crammed 200 soldiers...

139
00:11:24,142 --> 00:11:26,186
Yeah, we know the story, Daddy.

140
00:11:26,186 --> 00:11:28,105
...into that tiny social hall

141
00:11:28,105 --> 00:11:30,399
and the first person
to walk onstage

142
00:11:30,399 --> 00:11:32,985
is your mother,
and she starts to dance.

143
00:11:32,985 --> 00:11:38,156
You know, it had been months
since we'd even seen a woman

144
00:11:38,156 --> 00:11:40,033
and here's this blonde angel.

145
00:11:40,033 --> 00:11:41,493
Blonde bombshell.

146
00:11:43,328 --> 00:11:46,666
And the men are literally
holding their breath.

147
00:11:46,666 --> 00:11:48,250
Holding their breath for you.

148
00:11:48,250 --> 00:11:49,334
You hear that?

149
00:11:50,377 --> 00:11:51,378
Mm-hmm.

150
00:11:51,378 --> 00:11:53,630
And I turned to my buddies
and I said...

151
00:11:53,630 --> 00:11:55,799
"I will not leave France
without her."

152
00:11:55,799 --> 00:11:57,134
And I didn't.

153
00:11:57,134 --> 00:11:58,760
You didn't.

154
00:11:58,760 --> 00:12:00,596
I didn't.

155
00:12:01,680 --> 00:12:03,849
Oh, shit! Oh, shit, the rug!

156
00:12:03,849 --> 00:12:04,851
Aw, Mom...

157
00:12:04,851 --> 00:12:06,018
I can't believe I did that.

158
00:12:06,018 --> 00:12:07,519
No, no, it's nothing.
It's nothing.

159
00:12:07,519 --> 00:12:09,021
Oh, Frankie, Frankie,
get a towel.

160
00:12:09,021 --> 00:12:10,023
- Yeah, yeah.
- Paula...

161
00:12:10,023 --> 00:12:11,024
Come on.

162
00:12:11,024 --> 00:12:14,861
My sweet embraceable you...

163
00:12:14,861 --> 00:12:16,862
Dance with me, Paula.

164
00:12:18,989 --> 00:12:21,783
Ah...

165
00:12:27,706 --> 00:12:29,541
Whenever I dance for you

166
00:12:29,541 --> 00:12:31,418
I get in trouble.

167
00:12:37,758 --> 00:12:39,551
Watch this.

168
00:12:41,803 --> 00:12:44,515
Oh!

169
00:12:44,515 --> 00:12:46,433
Frank! Wake up.

170
00:12:46,433 --> 00:12:47,518
Come on, let's go!

171
00:12:47,518 --> 00:12:49,395
Get up. Come on, come on.

172
00:12:49,395 --> 00:12:50,938
Frank, wake up.

173
00:12:50,938 --> 00:12:52,022
Dad...

174
00:12:52,022 --> 00:12:54,399
You don't have to go
to school today.

175
00:12:54,399 --> 00:12:55,484
It's okay.

176
00:12:55,484 --> 00:12:56,693
Why? Is it snowing?

177
00:12:56,693 --> 00:12:58,153
Do you have a black suit?

178
00:12:58,153 --> 00:13:00,489
I overslept again, huh?

179
00:13:00,489 --> 00:13:03,575
We have a very important
meeting in the city.

180
00:13:03,575 --> 00:13:05,452
Eat that.
Come on, come on, eat.

181
00:13:05,452 --> 00:13:07,079
Ma'am, open up.
Just open up, please!

182
00:13:07,079 --> 00:13:08,081
- Ma'am?
- It's important.

183
00:13:08,081 --> 00:13:09,248
What?

184
00:13:09,248 --> 00:13:10,874
Oh, gosh!

185
00:13:10,874 --> 00:13:12,209
We don't open
for half an hour!

186
00:13:12,209 --> 00:13:14,086
Open the door, please.
Just open the door.

187
00:13:14,086 --> 00:13:15,087
It's important.

188
00:13:15,087 --> 00:13:17,047
I-I'm sorry, we don't open
for half an hour.

189
00:13:17,047 --> 00:13:18,131
What's your name, ma'am?

190
00:13:18,131 --> 00:13:19,341
Darcy.

191
00:13:19,341 --> 00:13:20,467
Darcy. That's a pretty name.

192
00:13:20,467 --> 00:13:23,595
I'm in a bit of a fix.
I need a suit for my kid.

193
00:13:23,595 --> 00:13:24,763
This is my son Frank.

194
00:13:24,763 --> 00:13:25,764
Oh, hi.

195
00:13:25,764 --> 00:13:26,765
He needs a black suit.

196
00:13:26,765 --> 00:13:27,808
Black suit...

197
00:13:27,808 --> 00:13:28,851
There was a death in the family.

198
00:13:28,851 --> 00:13:30,686
My father, :5 years old,
war hero.

199
00:13:30,686 --> 00:13:31,687
Yeah?

200
00:13:31,687 --> 00:13:34,314
There's a funeral
this afternoon, military funeral

201
00:13:34,314 --> 00:13:36,984
planes flying overhead,
21-gun salute.

202
00:13:36,984 --> 00:13:37,985
Geez.

203
00:13:37,985 --> 00:13:40,863
Frank needs to borrow a suit
for a couple of hours.

204
00:13:40,863 --> 00:13:43,907
I'm sorry, we don't loan suits,
and we're not open.

205
00:13:43,907 --> 00:13:45,285
Darcy... Darcy, please.

206
00:13:45,285 --> 00:13:47,202
- Come back.

207
00:13:47,202 --> 00:13:49,288
Darcy... is this yours?

208
00:13:55,002 --> 00:13:57,754
I just found it
in the parking lot.

209
00:13:57,754 --> 00:13:59,923
Uh-huh.

210
00:13:59,923 --> 00:14:02,301
It must've slipped
right off your neck.

211
00:14:05,137 --> 00:14:07,097
Don't hit the curb.

212
00:14:12,144 --> 00:14:13,980
Now get out,
walk around the back

213
00:14:13,980 --> 00:14:15,772
and hold the door open for me.

214
00:14:25,282 --> 00:14:26,533
All right.

215
00:14:26,533 --> 00:14:27,784
What's next?

216
00:14:27,784 --> 00:14:29,162
Okay, stop grinning.

217
00:14:29,162 --> 00:14:32,623
When I get inside, you go back
to the front seat and wait.

218
00:14:32,623 --> 00:14:35,167
Even if a cop comes
and writes you a ticket

219
00:14:35,167 --> 00:14:37,211
you don't move the car,
understood?

220
00:14:37,211 --> 00:14:39,171
Dad, wha-what's all this for?

221
00:14:39,171 --> 00:14:41,256
You know why the Yankees
always win, Frank?

222
00:14:41,256 --> 00:14:42,925
'Cause they have Mickey Mantle?

223
00:14:42,925 --> 00:14:44,676
No, it's 'cause the other teams

224
00:14:44,676 --> 00:14:47,095
can't stop staring
at those damn pinstripes.

225
00:14:47,095 --> 00:14:48,138
Watch this.

226
00:14:48,138 --> 00:14:50,016
The manager
of Chase Manhattan Bank

227
00:14:50,016 --> 00:14:54,436
is about to open the door
for your father.

228
00:14:56,104 --> 00:14:58,525
Mr. Abagnale, um

229
00:14:58,525 --> 00:15:00,651
we don't usually
loan money to people

230
00:15:00,651 --> 00:15:03,029
who have unresolved business
with the IRs.

231
00:15:03,029 --> 00:15:04,905
That's a misunderstanding.

232
00:15:04,905 --> 00:15:06,615
I hired the wrong guy
to do my books.

233
00:15:06,615 --> 00:15:08,368
A mistake, I...
anybody could make it.

234
00:15:08,368 --> 00:15:10,703
I just need you guys
to help me weather the storm.

235
00:15:10,703 --> 00:15:15,207
sir, you're being investigated
by the government for tax fraud.

236
00:15:15,207 --> 00:15:17,334
My store is a landmark
in New Rochelle.

237
00:15:17,334 --> 00:15:19,253
I have customers
all over New York.

238
00:15:19,253 --> 00:15:21,880
Well, you're not a customer
at Chase Manhattan.

239
00:15:21,880 --> 00:15:23,006
We don't know you.

240
00:15:23,006 --> 00:15:24,800
I'm sure your bank
in New Rochelle

241
00:15:24,800 --> 00:15:26,385
they know you,
they could help you out.

242
00:15:26,385 --> 00:15:27,970
My bank went out of business.

243
00:15:27,970 --> 00:15:30,890
Banks like this one put
them out of business.

244
00:15:30,890 --> 00:15:33,058
Now, I know I made a mistake,
I admit that

245
00:15:33,058 --> 00:15:34,561
but these people want blood.

246
00:15:34,561 --> 00:15:35,562
They want my store.

247
00:15:35,562 --> 00:15:37,479
They've threatened
to put me in jail.

248
00:15:39,147 --> 00:15:40,732
This is America, right?

249
00:15:40,732 --> 00:15:41,775
I'm not a criminal.

250
00:15:41,775 --> 00:15:43,318
I'm a Medal of Honor winner

251
00:15:43,318 --> 00:15:46,113
a lifetime member
of the New Rochelle Rotary Club.

252
00:15:46,113 --> 00:15:49,075
All I'm asking is for you
to help me beat these guys.

253
00:15:49,075 --> 00:15:51,660
It's not a question
of winning and losing.

254
00:15:51,660 --> 00:15:53,203
It's a question of risk.

255
00:15:53,203 --> 00:15:55,914
You're the largest bank
in the world.

256
00:15:55,914 --> 00:15:57,541
Where's the fu...

257
00:15:57,541 --> 00:16:00,252
Whe... Where's the risk?

258
00:16:05,424 --> 00:16:08,427
Dad, how could you just let him
take our car like that?

259
00:16:08,427 --> 00:16:10,429
He didn't take anything.
We took him.

260
00:16:10,429 --> 00:16:11,597
He overpaid by $500.

261
00:16:11,597 --> 00:16:14,892
Come on, Frank.

262
00:16:14,892 --> 00:16:16,310
Let's return the suit.

263
00:16:24,193 --> 00:16:25,694
This place is good.

264
00:16:25,694 --> 00:16:27,654
It's small but, you know

265
00:16:27,654 --> 00:16:30,240
it's going to be
a lot less work.

266
00:16:30,240 --> 00:16:31,783
A lot less work for you.

267
00:16:54,973 --> 00:16:56,308
Hey, Dad.

268
00:16:56,308 --> 00:16:58,268
Hi.

269
00:17:01,855 --> 00:17:03,941
Where's your mother?

270
00:17:03,941 --> 00:17:05,609
I don't know.

271
00:17:05,609 --> 00:17:09,696
she said something
about going to look for a job.

272
00:17:10,864 --> 00:17:12,032
What's she gonna be

273
00:17:12,032 --> 00:17:14,451
a shoe salesman
at a centipede farm?

274
00:17:20,082 --> 00:17:21,583
What are you doing?

275
00:17:23,168 --> 00:17:25,462
You want some pancakes?

276
00:17:25,462 --> 00:17:26,463
For dinner?

277
00:17:26,463 --> 00:17:28,341
On my son's 16th birthday?

278
00:17:28,341 --> 00:17:30,467
We're not gonna eat pancakes.

279
00:17:33,053 --> 00:17:35,681
Come on, why are you looking
at me like that?

280
00:17:35,681 --> 00:17:36,974
You thought I forgot?

281
00:17:36,974 --> 00:17:40,018
- I didn't think you forgot.
- I opened a checking account

282
00:17:40,018 --> 00:17:41,854
in your name.

283
00:17:41,854 --> 00:17:44,022
I put $25 in the account

284
00:17:44,022 --> 00:17:45,358
so you can buy
whatever you want.

285
00:17:45,358 --> 00:17:46,359
Don't tell your mother.

286
00:17:46,359 --> 00:17:47,484
I won't.

287
00:17:47,484 --> 00:17:48,610
Thanks, Dad.

288
00:17:48,610 --> 00:17:49,695
Yep.

289
00:17:49,695 --> 00:17:52,781
Didn't that bank turn you down
for a loan, though?

290
00:17:52,781 --> 00:17:54,449
Yes, they all turned me down.

291
00:17:55,868 --> 00:17:58,161
Then why you opening
a banking account with them?

292
00:17:58,161 --> 00:18:00,455
Well, because one day,
you'll want something

293
00:18:00,455 --> 00:18:02,374
from these people...
a house, a car.

294
00:18:02,374 --> 00:18:05,335
They have all the money.

295
00:18:08,130 --> 00:18:11,049
There's 50 checks there, Frank

296
00:18:11,049 --> 00:18:15,679
which means, from this day on...

297
00:18:15,679 --> 00:18:17,890
you're in their little club.

298
00:18:17,890 --> 00:18:21,351
I'm in their little club.

299
00:18:21,351 --> 00:18:23,478
You got that, you got it all.

300
00:18:23,478 --> 00:18:27,900
It's even got my name there,
huh?

301
00:18:27,900 --> 00:18:29,234
To the moon.

302
00:18:29,234 --> 00:18:34,198
- To the moon!
- To the moon.

303
00:18:42,539 --> 00:18:45,418
see that? It's just a school.

304
00:18:45,418 --> 00:18:47,211
No different than Westbourne.

305
00:18:47,211 --> 00:18:51,006
Ma... you said you were
going to quit.

306
00:18:54,176 --> 00:18:57,721
Frankie, you don't have
to wear the uniform here.

307
00:18:57,721 --> 00:19:00,682
Why don't you take off
your jacket?

308
00:19:00,682 --> 00:19:03,685
I'm used to it.

309
00:19:08,106 --> 00:19:09,233
Excuse me.

310
00:19:09,233 --> 00:19:10,234
Oh, yes?

311
00:19:10,234 --> 00:19:12,111
Do you know
where room 17 French is?

312
00:19:12,111 --> 00:19:13,862
Yeah, it's...

313
00:19:22,120 --> 00:19:24,039
But you frickin' killed him.

314
00:19:27,626 --> 00:19:29,753
You selling encyclopedias?

315
00:19:29,753 --> 00:19:33,215
Yeah, he looks like
a substitute teacher.

316
00:19:48,021 --> 00:19:50,190
Quiet down, people!

317
00:19:50,190 --> 00:19:52,485
My name is Mr. Abagnale!

318
00:19:52,485 --> 00:19:55,737
That's Abagnale, not Abagnahlee

319
00:19:55,737 --> 00:19:59,158
not Abagnaylee, but Abagnale!

320
00:19:59,158 --> 00:20:01,410
Now, somebody please tell me
where you left off

321
00:20:01,410 --> 00:20:03,412
in your textbooks.

322
00:20:04,872 --> 00:20:06,623
Excuse me, people,
if I need to ask again

323
00:20:06,623 --> 00:20:08,292
I'm going to write up
the entire class.

324
00:20:08,292 --> 00:20:10,627
Take your seats!

325
00:20:14,798 --> 00:20:16,383
Chapter seven.

326
00:20:16,383 --> 00:20:18,844
Will you please open your
textbooks to, uh, chapter eight

327
00:20:18,844 --> 00:20:21,471
and we'll get started?

328
00:20:21,471 --> 00:20:22,973
Excuse me,
what's your name?

329
00:20:22,973 --> 00:20:24,141
Brad.

330
00:20:24,141 --> 00:20:27,436
Brad, why don't you get up here
in front of the class here

331
00:20:27,436 --> 00:20:30,606
and read conversation
number five?

332
00:20:33,358 --> 00:20:36,153
:
"Les Francais sonts

333
00:20:36,153 --> 00:20:38,155
"uh, generalement

334
00:20:38,155 --> 00:20:40,991
- "dans leur pais que...

335
00:20:40,991 --> 00:20:43,870
presque tout le monde
a cette impression..."

336
00:20:43,870 --> 00:20:45,287
They sent for me.

337
00:20:45,287 --> 00:20:48,540
They said they needed a sub
for Roberta.

338
00:20:48,540 --> 00:20:51,376
I came all the way
from-from Dixon.

339
00:20:51,376 --> 00:20:53,879
Well, uh,
I always sub for Roberta.

340
00:20:53,879 --> 00:20:55,672
Excuse me,
why aren't you reading?

341
00:20:56,924 --> 00:21:01,845
I'll never come back to-to
Bellarmine Jefferson again!

342
00:21:01,845 --> 00:21:03,764
- You tell them not to call me!

343
00:21:03,764 --> 00:21:05,891
What do they think,
it's easy for a woman my age

344
00:21:05,891 --> 00:21:08,185
and all the money that it costs
to travel?

345
00:21:08,185 --> 00:21:09,436
I tell you,
they don't give a damn.

346
00:21:10,521 --> 00:21:11,647
Mr. And Mrs. Abagnale

347
00:21:11,647 --> 00:21:14,775
this is not a question
of your son's attendance.

348
00:21:14,775 --> 00:21:17,945
I regret to inform you that,
for the past week

349
00:21:17,945 --> 00:21:21,532
Frank has been teaching
Mrs. Glasser's French class.

350
00:21:22,908 --> 00:21:24,243
He what?

351
00:21:24,243 --> 00:21:28,372
Your son has been pretending
to be a substitute teacher

352
00:21:28,372 --> 00:21:32,751
lecturing the students,
uh, giving out homework.

353
00:21:32,751 --> 00:21:35,754
Mrs. Glasser has been ill
and there was

354
00:21:35,754 --> 00:21:39,258
some confusion
with the real sub.

355
00:21:39,258 --> 00:21:42,803
Your son held a teacher-parent
conference yesterday

356
00:21:42,803 --> 00:21:45,264
and was planning
a class field trip

357
00:21:45,264 --> 00:21:47,808
to a French bread factory
in Trenton.

358
00:21:47,808 --> 00:21:51,436
Do you see the problem we have?

359
00:21:51,436 --> 00:21:53,438
- Mrs. Davenport?
- Yeah.

360
00:21:53,438 --> 00:21:55,941
Uh, I have a note to miss fifth
and sixth period today.

361
00:21:55,941 --> 00:21:56,943
Doctor's appointment.

362
00:21:56,943 --> 00:21:58,610
- One moment.

363
00:21:58,610 --> 00:22:00,404
I'll be right with you.

364
00:22:00,404 --> 00:22:01,405
Hey...

365
00:22:01,405 --> 00:22:02,406
Yes?

366
00:22:02,406 --> 00:22:03,615
You should fold it.

367
00:22:03,615 --> 00:22:04,741
What?

368
00:22:04,741 --> 00:22:06,410
That note. It's a fake, right?

369
00:22:06,410 --> 00:22:07,744
You should fold it.

370
00:22:07,744 --> 00:22:09,538
It's... It's a note from my mom.

371
00:22:09,538 --> 00:22:11,290
I have a doctor's appointment.

372
00:22:11,290 --> 00:22:14,168
Yeah, but there's no crease
in the paper.

373
00:22:14,168 --> 00:22:16,253
When your mom hands you a note
to miss school

374
00:22:16,253 --> 00:22:17,462
the first thing you do is,
you fold it

375
00:22:17,462 --> 00:22:19,423
and you put it in your pocket.

376
00:22:19,423 --> 00:22:22,593
I mean, if it's real,
where's the crease?

377
00:22:32,311 --> 00:22:33,645
Frankie.

378
00:22:49,244 --> 00:22:51,205
Ma, I'm home.

379
00:22:53,332 --> 00:22:55,334
Oh, you remember
that girl Joanna

380
00:22:55,334 --> 00:22:57,503
I was telling you about?

381
00:22:57,503 --> 00:23:00,672
I asked her out today.

382
00:23:00,672 --> 00:23:03,008
Think we're going to go
to the Junior Prom.

383
00:23:05,010 --> 00:23:06,887
Ma, is this
my driver's license?

384
00:23:06,887 --> 00:23:08,681
That's all there is,
two bedrooms.

385
00:23:08,681 --> 00:23:11,099
Oh, Frankie.

386
00:23:11,099 --> 00:23:13,060
You remember Dad's friend?

387
00:23:13,060 --> 00:23:15,020
Jack Barnes?

388
00:23:15,020 --> 00:23:16,313
From the club.

389
00:23:16,313 --> 00:23:17,356
Hello.

390
00:23:17,356 --> 00:23:19,316
He came by looking
for your father.

391
00:23:19,316 --> 00:23:22,069
I was giving him
a tour of the apartment.

392
00:23:22,069 --> 00:23:25,113
It's very, uh, uh,
spacious, Paula.

393
00:23:30,661 --> 00:23:32,955
Dad's at the store.

394
00:23:32,955 --> 00:23:35,999
so, Frank...
you're getting to look

395
00:23:35,999 --> 00:23:38,877
more like your old man
every day.

396
00:23:41,505 --> 00:23:43,924
Thanks for the sandwich, Paula.

397
00:23:43,924 --> 00:23:45,718
I'll see you later, eh?

398
00:23:45,718 --> 00:23:47,719
Wait.

399
00:23:51,890 --> 00:23:54,059
Is this yours?

400
00:23:54,059 --> 00:23:56,770
Oh.

401
00:23:56,770 --> 00:23:58,480
Well, thanks, Frank.

402
00:23:58,480 --> 00:24:00,858
Uh, that's the President's pin.

403
00:24:00,858 --> 00:24:03,277
I'd be in deep trouble
if I lost that.

404
00:24:09,366 --> 00:24:11,451
I'll see you all later, eh?

405
00:24:21,003 --> 00:24:23,172
Are you hungry, Frankie?

406
00:24:23,172 --> 00:24:25,591
I'll make you a sandwich.

407
00:24:33,724 --> 00:24:36,935
Jack wanted to talk business
with your father.

408
00:24:36,935 --> 00:24:40,856
He thinks we should get a lawyer
and sue the government.

409
00:24:42,399 --> 00:24:45,319
That is not legal,
what they're doing to us.

410
00:24:48,447 --> 00:24:50,449
Why aren't you saying anything?

411
00:24:56,872 --> 00:25:01,543
You're not going to tell him...
are you?

412
00:25:01,543 --> 00:25:03,296
No.

413
00:25:03,296 --> 00:25:04,630
That's right.

414
00:25:04,630 --> 00:25:07,341
There's nothing to tell.

415
00:25:07,341 --> 00:25:09,051
I'm going out for a few hours

416
00:25:09,051 --> 00:25:12,179
to visit some old friends
from the tennis club and...

417
00:25:12,179 --> 00:25:15,807
when I get home,
we'll all have dinner together.

418
00:25:15,807 --> 00:25:17,810
Right?

419
00:25:17,810 --> 00:25:19,895
But you won't say anything

420
00:25:19,895 --> 00:25:23,316
because it's...
it's just silly, isn't it?

421
00:25:23,316 --> 00:25:25,317
How could we sue anybody?

422
00:25:29,321 --> 00:25:33,784
Oh... do you need
some money, Frankie?

423
00:25:33,784 --> 00:25:37,621
A few dollars to buy
some record albums?

424
00:25:37,621 --> 00:25:40,791
Here, take five dollars.

425
00:25:40,791 --> 00:25:42,793
Or-Or ten.

426
00:25:48,340 --> 00:25:50,300
You promised you were going
to quit.

427
00:26:09,820 --> 00:26:11,822
Ma, I'm home!

428
00:26:26,712 --> 00:26:28,255
Hey, hey!

429
00:26:28,255 --> 00:26:29,631
You... You stay away from me,
hear me?

430
00:26:29,631 --> 00:26:31,383
You stay away from me...
I don't know who you are

431
00:26:31,383 --> 00:26:32,676
but if you ever
come back here again...

432
00:26:32,676 --> 00:26:34,928
Frankie! Frank, Frank,
calm down, will you?

433
00:26:34,928 --> 00:26:36,221
I'm Dick Kesner.

434
00:26:36,221 --> 00:26:38,390
Now, I want you
to leave your things here

435
00:26:38,390 --> 00:26:40,475
and follow me
into the next room, okay?

436
00:26:40,475 --> 00:26:42,019
They're all waiting for you.

437
00:26:45,105 --> 00:26:46,690
You don't have to be scared.

438
00:26:46,690 --> 00:26:49,401
I'm right here, Frank.
I'll always be here.

439
00:26:49,401 --> 00:26:51,111
But there are laws.

440
00:26:51,111 --> 00:26:53,363
Everything in this country has
to be legal.

441
00:26:53,363 --> 00:26:56,408
so what we need to do is
make some decisions.

442
00:26:56,408 --> 00:26:58,076
That's what Mr. Kesner
is here for.

443
00:26:58,076 --> 00:27:00,413
Many times these decisions
are left up to the courts

444
00:27:00,413 --> 00:27:01,914
but that can be
very expensive, Frank

445
00:27:01,914 --> 00:27:03,582
people fighting
over their children.

446
00:27:03,582 --> 00:27:05,250
Nobody is fighting.

447
00:27:05,250 --> 00:27:07,878
Look at me, Frank.
Nobody is fighting.

448
00:27:10,297 --> 00:27:12,592
Dad, what's going on?

449
00:27:12,592 --> 00:27:14,134
Dad, what's going on?

450
00:27:16,762 --> 00:27:18,430
Do you remember your grandma,
Eve?

451
00:27:18,430 --> 00:27:20,140
she arrived this morning.

452
00:27:21,600 --> 00:27:23,143
Hello.

453
00:27:31,485 --> 00:27:34,696
Do you understand what
we're saying to you, Frank?

454
00:27:36,323 --> 00:27:40,285
Your father and I
are getting a divorce.

455
00:27:42,871 --> 00:27:44,122
Nothing's gonna change.

456
00:27:44,122 --> 00:27:45,832
We're still gonna see
each other.

457
00:27:45,832 --> 00:27:47,793
stop it, please, Frank.
Don't interrupt.

458
00:27:47,793 --> 00:27:50,129
Frank, you don't
have to read all of this.

459
00:27:50,129 --> 00:27:52,966
Most of it's for your parents...
boring adult business...

460
00:27:52,966 --> 00:27:55,759
but this paragraph right here,
this is important

461
00:27:55,759 --> 00:27:58,804
because it states
who you're gonna live with...

462
00:27:58,804 --> 00:28:00,806
after the divorce...

463
00:28:00,806 --> 00:28:02,766
whose custody you will be in.

464
00:28:02,766 --> 00:28:05,894
And there's a blank space
right here.

465
00:28:06,937 --> 00:28:08,397
And I want you to go
into the kitchen

466
00:28:08,397 --> 00:28:09,982
sit at the table

467
00:28:09,982 --> 00:28:11,692
and put a name down.

468
00:28:11,692 --> 00:28:13,026
You can take
as long as you want

469
00:28:13,026 --> 00:28:14,736
but when you come back
into this room...

470
00:28:14,736 --> 00:28:17,364
I want to see a name
on that line.

471
00:28:17,364 --> 00:28:21,159
Frank, just write down a name
and this will all be over.

472
00:28:21,159 --> 00:28:23,036
It's gonna be okay.

473
00:28:23,036 --> 00:28:24,831
FRANK:
Dad, what name?

474
00:28:24,831 --> 00:28:25,998
Your mother or your father.

475
00:28:25,998 --> 00:28:28,208
Just put the name there.
It's as simple as that.

476
00:28:28,208 --> 00:28:31,253
And don't look so scared.

477
00:28:31,253 --> 00:28:33,006
It's not a test.

478
00:28:33,006 --> 00:28:35,007
There's no wrong answer.

479
00:28:47,853 --> 00:28:48,854
Hi.

480
00:28:50,022 --> 00:28:51,565
One ticket to Grand Central,
please.

481
00:28:51,565 --> 00:28:54,860
That'll be $3.50, sir.

482
00:28:54,860 --> 00:28:57,696
Is it okay
if I write you a check?

483
00:29:02,701 --> 00:29:05,579
Carl, when do I get
to call my father?

484
00:29:05,579 --> 00:29:08,165
You can call him
when we get to New York.

485
00:29:08,165 --> 00:29:11,460
We leave for the airport
in seven hours.

486
00:29:11,460 --> 00:29:13,713
Until then, just sit there.

487
00:29:13,713 --> 00:29:16,507
Be quiet.

488
00:29:16,507 --> 00:29:18,884
You know, Carl,
on the other side of the hotel

489
00:29:18,884 --> 00:29:20,719
they got suites
that face the park.

490
00:29:21,845 --> 00:29:24,973
It's the best room
the FBI can afford.

491
00:29:27,351 --> 00:29:29,436
It's okay, I've stayed in worse.

492
00:29:29,436 --> 00:29:30,812
Mr. Mudrick...

493
00:29:30,812 --> 00:29:32,147
Mr. Mudrick, please.
You have to listen.

494
00:29:32,147 --> 00:29:33,941
I don't want to hear your story.

495
00:29:33,941 --> 00:29:35,734
This is two checks that bounced.

496
00:29:35,734 --> 00:29:37,528
You know how much trouble
I'm in?

497
00:29:37,528 --> 00:29:40,447
No, but listen, I'm telling you,
the bank, they made the mistake.

498
00:29:40,447 --> 00:29:41,990
I'll write you another check
right now!

499
00:29:41,990 --> 00:29:43,951
What, do I look like
I was born yesterday?

500
00:29:43,951 --> 00:29:45,827
Look, it's midnight,
Mr. Mudrick.

501
00:29:45,827 --> 00:29:46,828
Where am I gonna go?

502
00:29:46,828 --> 00:29:48,455
You're a goddamn kid.

503
00:29:48,455 --> 00:29:50,457
Go home.

504
00:30:38,088 --> 00:30:39,381
I mean, I hope you understand.

505
00:30:39,381 --> 00:30:41,216
My boss sent me to Brooklyn,
then Queens.

506
00:30:41,216 --> 00:30:42,676
Now he wants me in Long Island

507
00:30:42,676 --> 00:30:45,138
to take a few clients out
for a night on the town.

508
00:30:45,138 --> 00:30:49,099
I'm sorry, but we're not allowed
to cash checks from other banks.

509
00:30:49,099 --> 00:30:51,018
How would we know
if they were any good?

510
00:30:51,018 --> 00:30:52,769
What did you say your name was?

511
00:30:52,769 --> 00:30:53,937
Ashley.

512
00:30:53,937 --> 00:30:57,065
Ashley... you know what I found
on the sidewalk out there?

513
00:31:02,529 --> 00:31:04,698
Must've slipped
right off your neck.

514
00:31:09,161 --> 00:31:12,873
Is there something
I can help you with, son?

515
00:31:12,873 --> 00:31:15,334
Well, you see, it's my
grandmother's birthday next week

516
00:31:15,334 --> 00:31:17,336
and I want to get her
something extra-special.

517
00:31:17,336 --> 00:31:19,046
Please, I mean

518
00:31:19,046 --> 00:31:21,507
it's my midterm next week
and my books were stolen.

519
00:31:22,633 --> 00:31:24,551
Please, it's just five dollars.

520
00:31:24,551 --> 00:31:26,178
No one would have to know.

521
00:31:26,178 --> 00:31:28,847
I'm sorry,
but we are not allowed

522
00:31:28,847 --> 00:31:32,184
to take checks
from people we don't know.

523
00:32:13,892 --> 00:32:15,811
Pleasure to have you back,
Captain Carlson.

524
00:32:15,811 --> 00:32:17,230
What do you think, Angelo?

525
00:32:17,230 --> 00:32:18,856
The tomatoes are ripe
this afternoon.

526
00:32:26,822 --> 00:32:27,990
Well, what have we here?

527
00:32:27,990 --> 00:32:30,033
- Can I have your autograph?
- You betcha.

528
00:32:30,033 --> 00:32:32,077
- Can I have your autograph, too?
- You gonna be a pilot?

529
00:32:32,077 --> 00:32:34,246
- Mm-hmm!
- All right, then.

530
00:32:34,246 --> 00:32:35,747
There you go.
Work hard in school.

531
00:32:35,747 --> 00:32:37,124
Dear Dad:

532
00:32:37,124 --> 00:32:39,751
I have decided to become
an airline pilot.

533
00:32:39,751 --> 00:32:42,088
I have applied
to all the big airlines

534
00:32:42,088 --> 00:32:45,215
and I have several
promising interviews lined up.

535
00:32:45,215 --> 00:32:46,967
How's Mom?

536
00:32:46,967 --> 00:32:48,969
Have you called her lately?

537
00:32:48,969 --> 00:32:51,054
Love, your son, Frank.

538
00:32:54,433 --> 00:32:56,268
Hello.

539
00:32:56,268 --> 00:32:58,605
I'm Frank Black
from Murrow High school

540
00:32:58,605 --> 00:33:01,231
and I have an appointment
with Mr. Morgan.

541
00:33:01,231 --> 00:33:02,524
You're the young man

542
00:33:02,524 --> 00:33:04,444
who's writing the article
for the school paper.

543
00:33:04,444 --> 00:33:05,945
Yes, ma'am, that's me.
I want to know

544
00:33:05,945 --> 00:33:07,947
everything there is to know
about being a pilot.

545
00:33:07,947 --> 00:33:10,283
What airports
does Pan Am fly to?

546
00:33:10,283 --> 00:33:12,326
What does a pilot make
in a year?

547
00:33:12,326 --> 00:33:13,744
And who tells them
where they're gonna fly to?

548
00:33:13,744 --> 00:33:14,953
Whoa, whoa, whoa. Slow down.

549
00:33:14,953 --> 00:33:16,163
Just take 'em one at a time.

550
00:33:16,163 --> 00:33:17,372
All right. What does it mean

551
00:33:17,372 --> 00:33:18,957
when one pilot says
to another pilot:

552
00:33:18,957 --> 00:33:20,501
"What kind of equipment
are you on?"

553
00:33:20,501 --> 00:33:23,045
They just want to know what kind
of aircraft you're flying:

554
00:33:23,045 --> 00:33:24,464
Is it a DC-:707,
Constellation?

555
00:33:24,464 --> 00:33:27,132
And what about those I.D. badges
that I've seen pilots wear?

556
00:33:27,132 --> 00:33:29,343
Well, every pilot has to have
two things with him

557
00:33:29,343 --> 00:33:31,595
at all times: One is
his airline personnel badge...

558
00:33:31,595 --> 00:33:33,597
Looks just like this one here,
from Pan Am...

559
00:33:33,597 --> 00:33:36,225
the other one is
their FAA license...

560
00:33:36,225 --> 00:33:38,644
and that looks just like this.

561
00:33:38,644 --> 00:33:42,982
Oh. Sir, do you think I can
make a copy of this

562
00:33:42,982 --> 00:33:44,191
to put into my article?

563
00:33:44,191 --> 00:33:45,985
Oh, Frank,
you can have that one.

564
00:33:45,985 --> 00:33:47,402
It's three years expired.

565
00:33:47,402 --> 00:33:48,987
Aw, thanks!
And what about your I.D. badge?

566
00:33:48,987 --> 00:33:50,531
You have an extra one
I could borrow?

567
00:33:50,531 --> 00:33:52,533
Oh, no, I can't help you there.

568
00:33:52,533 --> 00:33:54,494
Those are special-ordered
from Polaroid.

569
00:33:54,494 --> 00:33:55,995
The only way
to get one of those

570
00:33:55,995 --> 00:33:58,622
is to become a real live pilot
for Pan American Airways.

571
00:34:03,460 --> 00:34:05,087
Pan Am, may I help you?

572
00:34:05,087 --> 00:34:06,547
Yeah, hello.

573
00:34:06,547 --> 00:34:07,631
I'm calling about a uniform.

574
00:34:07,631 --> 00:34:09,383
Hold for Purchasing.

575
00:34:09,383 --> 00:34:10,384
Thank you.

576
00:34:10,384 --> 00:34:11,760
Purchasing.

577
00:34:11,760 --> 00:34:13,262
Hi. I'm a copilot

578
00:34:13,262 --> 00:34:14,555
based out of san Francisco.

579
00:34:14,555 --> 00:34:16,473
I flew a flight
into New York last night

580
00:34:16,473 --> 00:34:17,516
but the problem is

581
00:34:17,516 --> 00:34:19,643
I'm headed out to, uh, Paris
in three hours.

582
00:34:19,643 --> 00:34:21,520
How can we help you?

583
00:34:21,520 --> 00:34:22,856
I sent my uniform

584
00:34:22,856 --> 00:34:24,731
to be cleaned through the hotel

585
00:34:24,731 --> 00:34:27,109
and I... I guess
they must have lost it.

586
00:34:27,109 --> 00:34:29,653
They lost a uniform.
Happens all the time.

587
00:34:29,653 --> 00:34:31,697
Go down to the Well-Built
Uniform Company

588
00:34:31,697 --> 00:34:32,781
at Ninth and Broadway.

589
00:34:32,781 --> 00:34:34,241
They're our uniform supplier.

590
00:34:34,241 --> 00:34:35,951
I'll tell Mr. Rosen
you're coming.

591
00:34:35,951 --> 00:34:38,203
You look too young
to be a pilot.

592
00:34:38,203 --> 00:34:39,955
I'm a copilot.

593
00:34:39,955 --> 00:34:41,623
Why so nervous?

594
00:34:41,623 --> 00:34:43,500
How would you feel if you, uh

595
00:34:43,500 --> 00:34:46,044
Lost your uniform
first week on the job?

596
00:34:46,044 --> 00:34:47,713
Relax.

597
00:34:47,713 --> 00:34:50,257
Pan Am's got lots of uniforms.

598
00:34:50,257 --> 00:34:52,552
It's gonna be $164.

599
00:34:52,552 --> 00:34:54,888
Great. I'II, uh,
I'll write you a check.

600
00:34:54,888 --> 00:34:57,014
sorry, no checks, no cash.

601
00:34:57,014 --> 00:34:59,183
You'll have to fill in
your employee I.D. number

602
00:34:59,183 --> 00:35:00,309
and then I'll bill Pan Am.

603
00:35:00,309 --> 00:35:02,227
They'll take it out
of your next paycheck.

604
00:35:04,396 --> 00:35:06,106
Even better.

605
00:35:08,066 --> 00:35:10,069
Dear Dad:

606
00:35:10,069 --> 00:35:13,030
You always told me that an
honest man has nothing to fear.

607
00:35:13,030 --> 00:35:15,073
So I'm trying my best
not to be afraid.

608
00:35:15,073 --> 00:35:19,077
I'm sorry I ran away,
but you don't have to worry.

609
00:35:19,077 --> 00:35:21,246
I'm gonna get it all back
now, Daddy.

610
00:35:21,246 --> 00:35:24,082
I promise.
I'm going to get it all back.

611
00:35:29,838 --> 00:35:31,924
Are you a real live pilot?

612
00:35:31,924 --> 00:35:34,092
I sure am, little lady.
What's your name?

613
00:35:34,092 --> 00:35:35,093
Celine.

614
00:35:35,093 --> 00:35:37,012
Celine, it's a pleasure
to meet you.

615
00:35:37,012 --> 00:35:39,056
It's a pleasure
to meet you, too.

616
00:35:42,684 --> 00:35:45,479
That's fifty, seventy

617
00:35:45,479 --> 00:35:49,441
eighty, ninety,
one hundred dollars.

618
00:35:49,441 --> 00:35:51,360
You have yourself a great time
in Paris.

619
00:35:51,360 --> 00:35:52,986
I always do.

620
00:35:52,986 --> 00:35:55,989
Excuse me.

621
00:35:55,989 --> 00:35:58,492
I'm John Modiger.
I manage this branch.

622
00:35:58,492 --> 00:36:00,118
I want to thank you
for coming in

623
00:36:00,118 --> 00:36:01,119
and using our institution.

624
00:36:01,119 --> 00:36:02,204
Well, it's a pleasure
to meet you, John.

625
00:36:02,204 --> 00:36:03,372
I'll be back again.

626
00:36:03,372 --> 00:36:04,373
Good.

627
00:36:04,373 --> 00:36:05,958
Have you stayed with us before?

628
00:36:05,958 --> 00:36:09,295
Uh, no, I've been primarily
based on the West Coast.

629
00:36:09,295 --> 00:36:12,714
Is it all right if I write you
a check for the room?

630
00:36:12,714 --> 00:36:13,799
No problem, sir.

631
00:36:13,799 --> 00:36:15,801
Great.

632
00:36:23,141 --> 00:36:24,309
Uh...

633
00:36:24,309 --> 00:36:25,394
I was also wondering

634
00:36:25,394 --> 00:36:27,312
if I could write you
a personal check?

635
00:36:27,312 --> 00:36:30,816
For airline personnel, we cash
personal checks up to $100.

636
00:36:30,816 --> 00:36:32,776
Payroll checks
we cash up to $300.

637
00:36:34,194 --> 00:36:36,989
Did you say $300
for a payroll check?

638
00:36:41,618 --> 00:36:43,997
Dear Dad:

639
00:36:43,997 --> 00:36:46,623
I've decided to become a pilot
for Pan American Airways...

640
00:36:46,623 --> 00:36:50,043
the most trusted name
in the skies.

641
00:36:50,043 --> 00:36:52,254
They've accepted me
into their training program

642
00:36:52,254 --> 00:36:54,256
and told me that if I work hard

643
00:36:54,256 --> 00:36:56,176
I should earn my wings
real soon.

644
00:36:56,176 --> 00:36:58,886
Please get in touch with Joanna
Carlton from the tenth grade.

645
00:36:58,886 --> 00:37:02,306
Tell her I'm sorry that I could
not take her to the Junior Prom.

646
00:37:02,306 --> 00:37:04,308
Love, your son, Frank.

647
00:37:43,180 --> 00:37:44,306
Hello, how are you?

648
00:37:44,306 --> 00:37:45,474
Fine, thank you.

649
00:37:45,474 --> 00:37:47,476
I have a payroll check here
I'd like to cash.

650
00:37:47,476 --> 00:37:49,144
Certainly.

651
00:37:49,144 --> 00:37:50,145
Thank you.

652
00:37:51,730 --> 00:37:53,233
Uh, excuse me.

653
00:37:53,233 --> 00:37:55,192
I'm sure you hear this
all the time

654
00:37:55,192 --> 00:37:57,486
but you have the most
beautiful eyes I have ever seen.

655
00:37:59,238 --> 00:38:00,697
Yeah, I do get that
all the time.

656
00:38:02,741 --> 00:38:04,368
How would you like it?

657
00:38:19,258 --> 00:38:21,468
Oh, I'm sorry, sir.

658
00:38:21,468 --> 00:38:24,596
We won't have any cash until
the banks open in an hour

659
00:38:24,596 --> 00:38:26,348
but, uh, I'm sure

660
00:38:26,348 --> 00:38:28,600
they can cash your check
at the airport.

661
00:38:28,600 --> 00:38:30,102
The airport?

662
00:38:30,102 --> 00:38:32,521
Who cashes checks
at the airport?

663
00:38:32,521 --> 00:38:33,605
Well, the airlines, sir.

664
00:38:33,605 --> 00:38:36,066
They've always taken care
of their own.

665
00:38:44,575 --> 00:38:45,659
Hello.

666
00:38:45,659 --> 00:38:47,327
Hi. Are you deadheading?

667
00:38:47,327 --> 00:38:49,121
What?

668
00:38:49,121 --> 00:38:51,123
Are you my deadhead to Miami?

669
00:38:51,123 --> 00:38:53,500
Mi...?

670
00:38:53,500 --> 00:38:54,835
Yes, yes.

671
00:38:56,044 --> 00:38:58,463
Yeah, I'm the deadhead.
Here you go.

672
00:38:58,463 --> 00:39:02,009
You're a little late,
but the jump seat is open.

673
00:39:03,677 --> 00:39:04,678
You know

674
00:39:04,678 --> 00:39:06,305
it's been awhile
since I've done this.

675
00:39:06,305 --> 00:39:07,764
Which one's the jump seat again?

676
00:39:10,309 --> 00:39:11,977
Have a nice flight.

677
00:39:11,977 --> 00:39:14,188
Are you my deadhead?

678
00:39:16,481 --> 00:39:18,400
Frank, Captain Oliver.

679
00:39:18,400 --> 00:39:20,068
- John Larkin, the copilot.
- Hello.

680
00:39:20,068 --> 00:39:21,236
Fred Tulley, flight engineer.

681
00:39:21,236 --> 00:39:22,404
Frank Taylor, Pan Am.

682
00:39:22,404 --> 00:39:24,281
Thanks for giving me a lift,
boys.

683
00:39:24,281 --> 00:39:26,074
Go ahead and take a seat, Frank.

684
00:39:26,074 --> 00:39:27,242
We're about to push.

685
00:39:27,242 --> 00:39:29,536
What kind of equipment you on,
DC-:?

686
00:39:29,536 --> 00:39:31,246
Uh, 707.

687
00:39:31,246 --> 00:39:33,999
You turning around
on the redeye?

688
00:39:33,999 --> 00:39:36,126
Uh, I'm jumping puddles
for the next few months

689
00:39:36,126 --> 00:39:38,003
trying to earn my keep
running leapfrogs

690
00:39:38,003 --> 00:39:39,171
for the weak and weary.

691
00:39:39,171 --> 00:39:42,216
No shame in that.
We all did it.

692
00:39:48,722 --> 00:39:49,765
Have a seat.

693
00:39:49,765 --> 00:39:50,849
Thank you.

694
00:39:51,850 --> 00:39:54,228
Would you like a drink
after takeoff?

695
00:39:54,228 --> 00:39:56,146
M-Milk?

696
00:39:56,146 --> 00:39:57,356
0 knots.

697
00:39:57,356 --> 00:39:58,440
Check.

698
00:39:58,440 --> 00:39:59,858
V-one. Rotate.

699
00:40:02,903 --> 00:40:04,947
V-two. Positive rate.

700
00:40:04,947 --> 00:40:07,241
Gear up.

701
00:40:07,241 --> 00:40:08,700
Dear Dad:

702
00:40:08,700 --> 00:40:10,160
Today was graduation.

703
00:40:10,160 --> 00:40:11,870
I am now a copilot

704
00:40:11,870 --> 00:40:15,165
earning $1,400 a month
plus benefits

705
00:40:15,165 --> 00:40:17,209
and the best part is

706
00:40:17,209 --> 00:40:19,461
they tell me
my family can fly for free.

707
00:40:19,461 --> 00:40:22,798
So tell Mom to pack her bags
and buy a new swimsuit

708
00:40:22,798 --> 00:40:26,051
because I'm taking us all
to Hawaii for Christmas.

709
00:40:26,051 --> 00:40:27,427
I love you, Dad.

710
00:40:27,427 --> 00:40:29,513
Aloha, Frank.

711
00:40:29,513 --> 00:40:31,139
Hello, deadhead.

712
00:40:31,139 --> 00:40:32,140
Hello.

713
00:40:32,140 --> 00:40:33,767
Enjoying your free ride?

714
00:40:39,690 --> 00:40:42,359
Marci...

715
00:40:42,359 --> 00:40:44,361
did you drop this?

716
00:40:47,239 --> 00:40:48,782
Must've slipped
right off your neck.

717
00:40:48,782 --> 00:40:50,117
No...

718
00:40:51,827 --> 00:40:53,871
No!

719
00:40:53,871 --> 00:40:55,581
No...

720
00:40:55,581 --> 00:40:58,208
Yes, yes, yes, yes!

721
00:41:00,836 --> 00:41:02,004
Yes!

722
00:41:05,007 --> 00:41:09,052
Why are you stopping?

723
00:41:09,052 --> 00:41:12,472
I want to tell you something,
Marci.

724
00:41:12,472 --> 00:41:16,269
This is by far...

725
00:41:16,269 --> 00:41:20,898
the best date I have ever
been on.

726
00:41:40,501 --> 00:41:42,669
I'd like to open a Money Market,
please.

727
00:41:42,669 --> 00:41:44,671
Okay.

728
00:41:50,802 --> 00:41:54,641
Welcome to Miami Mutual Bank.
How may I help you?

729
00:41:54,641 --> 00:41:58,060
My name is Frank Taylor.
I'm a copilot for Pan Am.

730
00:41:58,060 --> 00:42:00,938
I'd like to cash
this check here, and then

731
00:42:00,938 --> 00:42:03,941
I'd like to take you out
for a steak dinner.

732
00:42:14,117 --> 00:42:16,203
And then we feed the checks
into the MICR machine

733
00:42:16,203 --> 00:42:17,496
which uses special ink

734
00:42:17,496 --> 00:42:19,998
to encode the account numbers
on the bottom of the checks.

735
00:42:21,834 --> 00:42:24,419
And where are these numbers?

736
00:42:24,419 --> 00:42:26,672
They're, um... right here.

737
00:42:26,672 --> 00:42:28,048
Right there?

738
00:42:28,048 --> 00:42:29,174
see?

739
00:42:31,635 --> 00:42:33,679
They're called routing numbers.

740
00:42:33,679 --> 00:42:35,889
so where do the checks
get routed to?

741
00:42:36,932 --> 00:42:39,309
You know, I don't exactly know.

742
00:42:39,309 --> 00:42:40,811
Nobody ever
asked me that before.

743
00:42:42,604 --> 00:42:44,273
Our next item up for bid

744
00:42:44,273 --> 00:42:46,608
is also from the Jersey
Central Bank foreclosure.

745
00:42:46,608 --> 00:42:47,818
This is a MICR encoder

746
00:42:47,818 --> 00:42:49,528
a machine used
to encode bank checks.

747
00:42:49,528 --> 00:42:50,821
Do I have an opening bid?

748
00:42:50,821 --> 00:42:52,865
Our unknown subject
is a paperhanger

749
00:42:52,865 --> 00:42:54,616
who started working
on the East Coast.

750
00:42:54,616 --> 00:42:56,785
In the last few weeks,
this unsub has developed

751
00:42:56,785 --> 00:42:59,371
a new form of check fraud
which I'm calling "the float."

752
00:42:59,371 --> 00:43:00,455
What he's doing is

753
00:43:00,455 --> 00:43:02,541
he's opening checking accounts
at various banks

754
00:43:02,541 --> 00:43:04,377
then changing the MICR ink
routing numbers

755
00:43:04,377 --> 00:43:05,752
at the bottom of those checks.

756
00:43:05,752 --> 00:43:06,920
Next slide, please.

757
00:43:08,213 --> 00:43:10,215
Next slide, please.

758
00:43:10,215 --> 00:43:11,717
Uh, the remote thing is broken.

759
00:43:11,717 --> 00:43:13,135
You'll have to do it by hand.

760
00:43:13,135 --> 00:43:14,428
You've gotta... try the...

761
00:43:14,428 --> 00:43:15,888
Agent Mullen, it should be...

762
00:43:15,888 --> 00:43:18,515
it should be the square button
just there by the side.

763
00:43:18,515 --> 00:43:19,725
This carousel doesn't work.

764
00:43:19,725 --> 00:43:21,226
It's a bad carousel
you got there.

765
00:43:21,226 --> 00:43:22,269
Thank you, Agent Mullen.

766
00:43:22,269 --> 00:43:23,520
Got to move it manually.

767
00:43:23,520 --> 00:43:26,064
This is a map of the 12 banks
of the U.s. Federal Reserve.

768
00:43:26,064 --> 00:43:28,025
slide.

769
00:43:29,193 --> 00:43:30,652
MICR scanners at every bank

770
00:43:30,652 --> 00:43:33,113
read these numbers
at the bottom of a check...

771
00:43:33,113 --> 00:43:34,406
slide...

772
00:43:34,406 --> 00:43:37,701
and then, ship that check off
to its corresponding branch.

773
00:43:37,701 --> 00:43:40,454
Carl, for those of us who are
unfamiliar with bank fraud

774
00:43:40,454 --> 00:43:42,247
you mind telling us
what the hell

775
00:43:42,247 --> 00:43:44,250
you're talking about?

776
00:43:44,250 --> 00:43:48,253
The East Coast branches are
numbered zero-one to zero-six.

777
00:43:48,253 --> 00:43:50,297
The central branch
is zero-seven, zero-eight

778
00:43:50,297 --> 00:43:51,423
so on, so forth.

779
00:43:51,423 --> 00:43:53,091
You mean those numbers
on the bottom of a check

780
00:43:53,091 --> 00:43:54,218
actually mean something?

781
00:43:54,218 --> 00:43:56,596
All of this was in the report
I filed two days ago.

782
00:43:56,596 --> 00:44:00,807
If you change...
a zero-two to a one-two

783
00:44:00,807 --> 00:44:03,685
that means that check,
which was cashed in New York

784
00:44:03,685 --> 00:44:05,354
does not go
to the New York Federal Branch

785
00:44:05,354 --> 00:44:06,605
but it is rerouted

786
00:44:06,605 --> 00:44:09,900
all the way to the san Francisco
Federal Branch.

787
00:44:09,900 --> 00:44:12,778
